{"study_accession":"PRJNA817828","secondary_study_accession":"SRP364823","title":"Impaired CD4+ T cell differentiation in HIV-1 infected patients receiving early ART","description":"Differentiation of CD4+ T naive (TN) into central memory (TCM) cells involves extensive molecular processes triggered by exposure to antigens. Recent WHO guidelines recommend antiretroviral therapy (ART) administration early during HIV-1 infection to avoid severe immunological complications. We compared the transcriptomes of CD4+ TN and TCM cells from early ART (EA) HIV-1 infected patients and matched controls (C).</p><p>Total RNA was isolated from sorted blood CD4+ TN and TCM cells from 13 EA patients and 15 age-matched controls. The comparison of CD4+ TN with TCM cells transcriptomes revealed differentially expressed genes (DEGs) within and between cell populations. Data were analyzed for significant predictor genes and gene ontology (GO) term enrichment. A database of transcription factors (TFs) was assembled by merging publicly available information and TFs regulatory network with their target genes visualized. Published T helper cell gene sets were used to analyze T helper signatures of TCM.</p><p>The comparison of protein coding genes between TCM and TN revealed 533 and 82 DEGs in C and EA, respectively. The most enriched GO term was 'cytokine mediated signaling pathway' in C and 'regulation of apoptotic process' in EA. A high degree of transcriptional complexity was detected during transition of CD4+ TN to TCM cells in controls involving over 70 TFs of which 20 are regulators of T cell differentiation (including TBX21, GATA3, RARA, FOXP3, RORC); in EA only 7 of 20 TFs were modulated with expression of GATA3, RARA, FOXP3, RORC remaining unchanged. Ten chemokine receptors important for homing CD4+ TCM cells to tissues were differentially expressed between TCM and TN cells of controls whereas only five were modulated in EA group. A network analysis of interactions between modulated TFs and target genes revealed that important regulatory interactions were missing in EA compared to control group.","pubmed":"","geo_series":"","n_samples":28,"study_labels":[],"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN26809517","study_accession":"PRJNA817828","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"EA","description":"EA","sex":"male","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2022-09-27","attributes":[["isolate","EA19"],["age","64"],["biomaterial_provider","Francesca Chiodi"],["sex","male"],["tissue","Blood"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR18365631","experiment_accession":"SRX14500133","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000","read_count":34696782,"base_count":8219826961,"first_public":"2022-09-27","experiment_title":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000 sequencing: RNA-seq of homo sapiens: peripheral CD4+ central memory T cells (early treated HIV-1)"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN26809518","study_accession":"PRJNA817828","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"EA","description":"EA","sex":"male","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2022-09-27","attributes":[["isolate","EA20"],["age","49"],["biomaterial_provider","Francesca Chiodi"],["sex","male"],["tissue","Blood"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR18365630","experiment_accession":"SRX14500134","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000","read_count":44793507,"base_count":10226384472,"first_public":"2022-09-27","experiment_title":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000 sequencing: RNA-seq of homo sapiens: peripheral CD4+ central memory T cells (early treated HIV-1)"}],"labels":[],"single_cell":false},{"sample_accession":"SAMN26809519","study_accession":"PRJNA817828","blood_class":"blood_cells","class_from":"sample","title":"EA","description":"EA","sex":"male","geo_sample":"","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public"